Verdict: PASS, with two bookkeeping problems for follow-up. It's written to session_plan/eval_task_2.md.

I judged this from the diff, the commit log and a search for #1196 references. I didn't run the binary or any extra tests; build and tests had already passed in the harness.

What changed: If the whole command is a single plain echo or printf, the safety checker now treats its quoted words as text, not a command. So echo "kill -9 1 at $(date)" is no longer refused. Commands inside $(…) or backticks are still checked, so echo "x $(kill -9 1) y" is still refused. The fix is in both checking passes: the raw-text one in safety.rs (+2 lines) and the parsed-command one in bash_parse.rs.

The cause wasn't the `$(...)`: The agent's census found 5 of 33 checks refused the $(date) form. The same 5 also refused plain trailing text like echo "kill -9 1 at now". So the fix targets "quoted text in an echo/printf" rather than substitutions.

It stays on the cautious side: These are all still refused, and tests assert each one:
• the output piped to another command (echo "..." | sh)
• a redirect (echo "x" > /etc/passwd)
• a wrapper or path (sudo echo, /bin/echo)
• command bodies (sh -c "...", eval "...")
• a substitution or backtick inside the quotes

Supporting changes:
• Tests: a permanent test checks that every safety check gives the same verdict across the quoted forms.
• Docs: docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md describes the new rule and its limits.
• Size entry: tests/module_size.rs now records safety.rs at 4816 lines.
• Fixtures: one fixture in tools_child_bash_tests.rs now pipes to cat so it is still flagged. Its expected result didn't change, and no test was deleted.

Bookkeeping problems:
• Empty commit body: the task asked for a list of every verdict that flipped between the old and new checker, plus "Part of #1202". Neither is in the commit.
• "Fixes #1196" is premature: the commit claims to close the issue, but the new docs say git commit -m "kill -9 1 at $(date)" can still be refused. The task's own rule was to title it "Part of #1196" if anything remained. That case should become a follow-up.
